      'remove_favorite': 'Remove from favorites',
      // AUTO GENERATED ID RECIPES
      'desc_key_0': 'Use water at 99Â°C and a medium-fine grind. Create a divot in the center of the coffee bed. Swirl the brewer during the bloom phase and after the final pour to ensure even extraction.\n\nEstimated Flavor Profile: Balanced extraction, optimal sweetness, and high clarity.',
      'desc_key_1': 'Use a coarse grind and 90-92Â°C water. Allow the bed to dry completely before proceeding to the next pour. The first 40% of the water influences acidity and sweetness, and the remaining 60% controls the strength.\n\nFlavor Profile: Clean cup, bright acidity, with a light, tea-like body.',
      'desc_key_2': 'Medium-fine grind. Water at 90-92Â°C. Pour water very slowly, only in a coin-sized area in the center. DO NOT let the water touch the filter paper. Keep the coffee dome intact.\n\nEstimated Flavor Profile: Very sweet (maximum sweetness), with a smooth and rich body, and muted acidity.',
      'desc_key_3': 'Use a low temperature (around 90Â°C) and a coarse grind. Pour slowly in a circular motion without vigorous agitation. A flat-bed dripper is recommended.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very bright acidity, a clean cup, and prominent fruity notes.',
      'desc_key_4': 'Make a divot in the center. Use a high temperature. Perform a \'Rao Spin\' (swirl the dripper in a circular motion) after the bloom and at the end to level the coffee bed and prevent channeling.\n\nEstimated Taste: Balanced extraction (high extraction yield), sweet, minimal astringency.',
      'desc_key_5': 'Classic Hario factory recipe. Very beginner-friendly. Pour slowly in a circular motion from the center outwards, then back to the center.\n\nFlavor Profile: Classic, medium body, prominent acidity, and strong brewing aroma.',
      'desc_key_6': 'God Recipe by Tetsu Kasuya. Temperature 90Â°C. OPEN the valve at the start (percolation). At 65 seconds, CLOSE the valve (immersion). OPEN the valve again at the end. Produces a perfectly balanced extraction.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very sweet, a unique blend of V60 clarity and immersion body.',
      'desc_key_7': 'Medium grind. Pour water first, then add the coffee grounds. Stir after a few moments, then let it steep.\n\nFlavor Profile: Full body, very balanced flavor, and minimal risk of over-extraction.',
      'desc_key_8': 'Medium grind (does not need to be too coarse). Brew for 4 minutes, then stir the coffee crust on the surface. Skim the foam. Wait another 5-8 minutes before pouring, and DO NOT press the plunger to the bottom.\n\nFlavor Profile: Full body yet very clean without grounds, thick texture, and balanced flavor.',
      'desc_key_9': 'Coarse grind. Pour all the water and stir evenly. Let it steep for 4 minutes, then press the plunger down slowly to the bottom.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very thick body, strong aroma, classic gritty texture.',
      'desc_key_10': 'Brew a very strong coffee concentrate. Mix it with steamed or heated milk in a 1:1 ratio.\n\nFlavor Profile: Warm, milky, savory milk notes with a strong coffee character.',
      'desc_key_11': 'Medium-fine grind. Stir immediately with a spoon at the beginning. Let it steep for 5 minutes. Press the plunger ONLY until it touches the water to keep the fine grounds trapped below.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very clean for a French Press, smooth body, slowly extracted sweetness.',
      'desc_key_12': 'Stir vigorously at the beginning and immediately skim the foam in the first minute for even extraction from the start.\n\nFlavor Profile: Thick body with minimal grit, cleaner taste than traditional methods.',
      'desc_key_13': 'Pre-infuse with room temperature water for 1 minute, then pour hot water. This reduces bitterness and enhances the coffee\'s natural sweetness.\n\nFlavor Profile: Reduces sharp acidity and bitterness, highlighting a smoother sweet profile.',
      'desc_key_14': 'Similar to cupping. Brew for 4 minutes, stir gently, skim the foam and crust, wait another 5 minutes before pressing.\n\nFlavor Profile: Nordic roast (light) characteristics, bright acidity, light body, more akin to a cupping bowl.',
      'desc_key_15': '[Method: Standard] Use a paper filter. Low water temperature (80Â°C). Stir quickly for 10 seconds, then press slowly. Do not press until it hisses.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very sweet, low acidity, thick body, similar to espresso concentrate.',
      'desc_key_16': '[Method: Standard] Medium-fine grind. Boiling water. Standard brew (non-inverted). Stir gently, then press slowly at 60 seconds.\n\nFlavor Profile: Clean cup, bright acidity, tastes similar to a V60 pour-over.',
      'desc_key_17': '[Method: Standard] More coffee-efficient. Standard position. Brew for 2 minutes, swirl slightly, let it rest for 30 seconds, then press slowly.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very efficient, sweet, full body, and even extraction without excessive sourness.',
      'desc_key_18': '[Method: Inverted] (MUST be Inverted). Pour water, stir vigorously with a spoon, then attach the cap. At 1:30, carefully flip the brewer onto a cup and press down.\n\nFlavor Profile: Strong body, bold taste, perfect for mixing with milk or drinking black.',
      'desc_key_19': '[Method: Inverted] Shorter brew time, inverted method. Flip at 1:00 and press for 30 seconds.\n\nFlavor Profile: Brighter acidity, clean cup.',
      'desc_key_20': '[Method: Standard] Brew a very strong coffee concentrate (Use a Prismo or Flow Control attachment), then immediately stir with cold milk and ice cubes in a glass.\n\nFlavor Profile: Smooth, creamy, and refreshing milk punch.',
      'desc_key_21': '[Method: Standard] 2023 World Aeropress Championship winning recipe. Low-temperature extraction with intense stirring, then bypassed with hot water at the end.\n\nFlavor Profile: Complex, sweet, bright fruity acidity but with a very silky texture.',
      'desc_key_22': '[Method: Standard] Long 9-minute steep with a V60 grind size. Produces a sweet and even extraction without bitterness.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very high extraction yield (high EY), maximum sweetness, no under-extracted sourness.',
      'desc_key_23': '[Method: Standard] Use inverted or standard method. Boiling water to extract coffee concentrate. Stir evenly, then press over a glass filled with ice chunks.\n\nFlavor Profile: Refreshing, strong initially but melts slowly (refreshing), sweet.',
      'desc_key_24': '[Method: Standard] Very fine grind with very little water, pressed as hard as possible. Produces a thick concentrate similar to espresso.\n\nFlavor Profile: Strong, punchy, creates faux crema (if pressed hard), great for mocktails or milk drinks.',
      'desc_key_25': 'Medium-coarse grind. Dark roast Robusta coffee. Tamp down with the press filter. Pour boiling water. The dripping process takes about 5-6 minutes.\n\nFlavor Profile: Strong bitterness, smokey, very thick (syrupy) and bold body.',
      'desc_key_26': 'A lighter variation. Use medium-fine grind and Arabica beans. Tamp lightly, drip for 4 minutes.\n\nFlavor Profile: Brighter acidity, lighter body.',
      'desc_key_27': 'Use a 1:15 ratio, similar to a standard pour-over, but brewed with the Vietnam Drip. Tamp very lightly.\n\nFlavor Profile: Clean cup, balanced, similar to a slow drip V60.',
      'desc_key_28': 'Modern approach for light roast coffee beans. Extracts sweet and sour flavors cleanly.\n\nFlavor Profile: Cleaner than traditional methods, acidity starts to shine, modern flavor complexity.',
      'desc_key_29': 'Use an Aeropress paper filter on top of the coffee bed before tamping for a more even water distribution.\n\nFlavor Profile: Higher extraction yield, more balanced.',
      'desc_key_30': 'Classic combination of Sweetened Condensed Milk (SCM) and Palm Sugar Syrup. SCM provides the signature \'creamy\' texture of Vietnam Drip, while palm sugar adds a sweet caramel aroma. Pour SCM and palm sugar into the bottom of the glass, brew slowly, stir well, then add ice.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very creamy, sweet palm sugar caramel blends with a strong and thick coffee flavor.',
      'desc_key_31': 'Place a paper filter (Aeropress size) at the bottom of the Phin brewer before adding coffee. Produces an incredibly clean brew with no fine grounds. (Optional: place another paper filter on top of the coffee before tamping for super even extraction).\n\nFlavor Profile: Much cleaner with no grit, reduced body but with clearer flavor clarity.',
      'desc_key_32': 'Designed for specialty coffee beans (Light Roast), the tamping process is less firm to allow optimal water flow.\n\nFlavor Profile: A longer ratio produces a lighter beverage, similar to a strong pour-over.',
      'desc_key_33': 'Rare method: Tamp the coffee with the filter press, pour bloom water, then tamp AGAIN (second tamp) before pouring the rest of the water. Results in a much slower drip rate.\n\nFlavor Profile: Incredible extraction intensity, like espresso, with a very thick body.',
      'desc_key_34': 'Temperature 93Â°C. At minute 4, push the back of a cupping spoon 3 times across the coffee surface (break the crust). Skim the remaining foam. Start slurping at minute 10 when the temperature drops.\n\nFlavor Profile: Pure flavor profile of the coffee beans for defect/quality evaluation purposes.',
      'desc_key_35': 'Home-style cupping. Use two spoons to skim the foam at minute 4.\n\nFlavor Profile: Pure coffee bean flavor without paper filter interference, perfect for comparing 2 different beans.',
      'desc_key_36': 'Focuses on evaluating the coffee flavor when it is very cold at minute 15. The initial phase is the same, but wait much longer.\n\nFlavor Profile: Assesses the coffee flavor at room temperature (highlighting sweetness or alternatively, defects/sourness).',
      'desc_key_37': 'No crust breaking process. The coffee grounds are left to sink slowly to the bottom of the glass on their own. Results in a very clean flavor evaluation.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very clean, slower extraction avoiding excessive astringency.',
      'desc_key_38': 'Medium-fine grind. Pour hot water over the coffee; the drips will immediately hit the ice cubes in the server to lock in volatile aromas.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very sharp, refreshing, and aromatic fruity acidity (floral/fruity).',
      'desc_key_39': 'Similar to Japanese Iced Coffee, but the ice cube ratio is adjusted and the pours are divided to maximize sweetness.\n\nFlavor Profile: Sweeter and more balanced, not overly acidic, with a slightly thicker body.',
      'desc_key_40': 'Adaptation of the 2024 World Brewers Cup 3rd Place recipe. Uses 4 pour phases with decreasing volumes at the end to prevent over-extraction. Produces a balanced and syrupy flavor.\n\nFlavor Profile: Layered flavor, high complexity, elegant aromatic sweetness.',
      'desc_key_41': 'High temperature (93Â°C). Very fast water flow (fast flow). Swirl the dripper quite aggressively at the beginning so all grounds are evenly wetted. Finishes very quickly in 2 minutes.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very transparent fruity acidity, light, similar to coffee-flavored sweet tea.',
      'desc_key_42': 'Consistent pours for flat-bed drippers. Highlights acidity and flavor clarity.\n\nFlavor Profile: Incredible sweetness thanks to the flat bed, consistent extraction, balanced.',
      'desc_key_43': 'Use 85Â°C water and quick center pours to avoid burnt and excessively bitter flavors in dark roast beans.\n\nFlavor Profile: Smooth bitterness, caramel sweetness, without excessive burnt/smokey notes.',
      'desc_key_44': 'Devil Recipe V60 Switch. OPEN the valve at the beginning. MUST use ROOM TEMPERATURE water (cold/normal) for the first 60ml bloom phase. For the rest, use boiling hot water for the immersion phase.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very bold, thick sweet extraction at the start, finished with immersion for body.',
      'desc_key_45': '[Method: Inverted] 2018 World Aeropress Championship winning recipe. Inverted position, vigorous stirring, resulting in a super fruity extraction.\n\nFlavor Profile: Vibrant, bright acidity, clean finish.',
      'desc_key_46': '[Method: Inverted] 2017 Championship recipe. Strong 1:5 ratio at the beginning, topped up with bypass water at the end. Inverted.\n\nFlavor Profile: Round body, balanced, highly recommended for natural processed coffees.',
      'desc_key_47': '[Method: Standard] Standard Aeropress (not inverted). 80Â°C water, let it drip slowly then press very slowly.\n\nFlavor Profile: Complex, dominantly sweet, juicy body.',
      'desc_key_48': '[Method: Standard] Uses a Prismo cap or Flow Control. Full immersion without being inverted.\n\nFlavor Profile: Like home espresso, strong, no premature water leakage.',
      'desc_key_49': '[Method: Standard] High concentrate extraction resembling espresso. Use Prismo/Flow Control and an espresso grind size.\n\nFlavor Profile: Bitter, thick, bold, perfect for mixing with brown sugar or milk.',
      'desc_key_50': '[Method: Standard] Wide ratio and coarse grind for a refreshing, tea-like coffee flavor.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very light, transparent, dominant floral/fruity notes like premium tea.',
      'desc_key_51': '[Method: Standard] Specifically for Robusta coffee. Short extraction with 85Â°C temperature so it\'s not too bitter.\n\nFlavor Profile: Classic Robusta bitterness balanced by a thick texture and sweet nutty notes.',
      'desc_key_52': 'Make a salt cream by frothing sweetened condensed milk, liquid milk/cream, and salt until foamy. Drip the coffee over it. Stir before drinking.\n\nFlavor Profile: An amazing combination of savory saltiness, thick sweetness, and bold coffee bitterness. Umami!',
      'desc_key_53': 'Whip free-range egg yolks with sweetened condensed milk and a little honey until it fluffs into a thick foam. Drip hot coffee over or under it.\n\nFlavor Profile: Very creamy, dessert-like (tiramisu), thick sweetness, without any eggy smell.',
      'desc_key_54': 'A unique Vietnamese combination. Add yogurt to the bottom of the glass, add condensed milk, brew coffee over it, then add ice cubes.\n\nFlavor Profile: Fresh tangy yogurt combined with creamy sweetness and coffee bitterness. Very refreshing.',
      'desc_key_55': 'Vietnam Drip specifically for Arabica beans. Lightly tamped, 92Â°C temperature for a thick yet fruity body.\n\nFlavor Profile: Signature Arabica fruity acidity, body is not as thick compared to Robusta.',
      'desc_key_56': 'Add cocoa powder or chocolate syrup into the condensed milk. Drip the coffee through the chocolate. Stir well.\n\nFlavor Profile: Intense dark chocolate, sweet, thick, like a chocolate coffee candy.',
      'desc_key_57': 'A variant of Vietnamese iced milk coffee (Báº¡c Xá»‰u) with added coconut milk. The proportion of milk and coconut dominates over the coffee for a sweet, savory, and creamy taste.\n\nFlavor Profile: Savory sweet coconut, very creamy, softer background coffee flavor.',
